    "lawCode": "Amd §§27-2701, 27-2703, 27-2705, 27-2707, 27-2709 & 27-2713, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5817\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to the recycling of film plastic\n\nPURPOSE:    To  help  support the New York City-wide recycling program\nstate wide to accept film plastic in their collection bins.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  This bill amends provisions  of  the  \"Plastic\nBag Reduction, Reuse and Recycling Act\" (the \"Act\"), which was enacted\nby  the  State  in 2008.   Specifically, the bill would require stores\nstate  wide  to  accept  film  plastic  in  their   collection   bins.\nSubdivision 2 of Section 27-2701 of the environmental conservation law\nis amended by adding film plastic to the definition of \"manufacturer.\"\nSection 27-2701 is amended by adding a new subdivision 7 defining film\nplastic. Article 27, Title 27 of the environmental conservation law is\namended  throughout  to  include  film  plastic  in  the  plastic  bag\nrecycling program.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  Kew York City's \"Plastic Carryout Bag and Film Plastic\nRecycling Law,\" signed into law in 2008,  differed  from  the  State's\n\"Plastic  Bad  Reduction,  Reuse and Recycling Act\" because it imposed\nstricter plastic bag collection requirements on  a  larger  number  of\nstores,  and  applied  to  film plastic as well as to plastic carryout\nbags. The changes proposed in this bill would align state law with the\nCity's preempted stricter standard  by  adding  film  plastic  to  the\nplastic bag recycling program that is already in place statewide.\n\nThese  changes  will  lead  to  greater  compliance with the recycling\nprogram by both retail stores and by consumers, who will have  greater\nopportunities to recycle their unwanted plastic bags and film plastic.\nAs  indicated  in the approval memo of the New York State \"Plastic Bag\nReduction, Reuse and Recycling Act\", these changes to  the  bill  will\nhelp  support  the  successful City-wide recycling program that was in\nplace before the State Act took effect.\n\nLEGISLATIVE  HISTORY:.     2012:   A10433   (Kavanagh)   Environmental\nConservation\n\nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:  None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect on March 1, 2014.\n\n",
